🧠 Core Concepts
Users, Groups, UID/GID
User: An account that can own files and run processes. Identified by UID (User ID).

Group: A collection of users. Identified by GID (Group ID). Every user has a primary group and can belong to supplementary groups.

Root: UID 0 — the superuser with all privileges.

System users: UID < 1000 (typically), used by services (e.g., www-data, sshd).

Regular users: UID ≥ 1000.

bash
id                    # Show current user's UID, GID, groups
id alice              # Show another user's info
/etc/passwd, /etc/shadow, /etc/group
File	Purpose	Format
/etc/passwd	User account info	username:x:UID:GID:comment:home:shell
/etc/shadow	Encrypted passwords & aging	username:hash:lastchg:min:max:warn:inactive:expire
/etc/group	Group definitions	groupname:x:GID:member1,member2
Note: The x in /etc/passwd means the password is stored in /etc/shadow.

bash
# View entries
grep alice /etc/passwd
sudo grep alice /etc/shadow
grep developers /etc/group
chmod
Symbolic mode:

Symbol	Meaning
u	User (owner)
g	Group
o	Others
a	All
+	Add permission
-	Remove permission
=	Set exactly
bash
chmod u+x file          # Add execute for owner
chmod go-w file         # Remove write for group and others
chmod a+r file          # Add read for all
Numeric (octal) mode:

Value	Permission
4	Read (r)
2	Write (w)
1	Execute (x)
0	None
Examples:

755 = rwxr-xr-x

644 = rw-r--r--

700 = rwx------

600 = rw-------

bash
chmod 755 script.sh
chmod 644 config.conf
chmod 700 private.key
chown & chgrp
bash
chown alice file.txt              # Change owner
chown alice:developers file.txt   # Change owner and group
chown -R alice:developers dir/    # Recursive
chgrp developers file.txt         # Change group only
umask
umask determines default permissions for newly created files and directories.

Files: base 666 – umask

Directories: base 777 – umask

bash
umask                    # Show current umask (e.g., 0022)
umask 027                # Set umask for current shell
Common umask values:

umask	File perms	Dir perms
022	644	755
027	640	750
077	600	700
sudo
sudo allows a permitted user to execute a command as another user (usually root) according to rules in /etc/sudoers.

bash
sudo command             # Run as root
sudo -u alice command    # Run as another user
sudo -i                  # Interactive root shell
sudo -l                  # List allowed commands
visudo                   # Safely edit /etc/sudoers
How it works:

User runs sudo.

System checks /etc/sudoers (and /etc/sudoers.d/) for permission.

If allowed, user authenticates with their own password (by default).

Command runs with target user's privileges.

Action is logged (usually to /var/log/auth.log or /var/log/secure).

SUID, SGID, Sticky Bit
Special Permission	Numeric	Symbolic	Effect
SUID	4	u+s	File executes with owner's privileges
SGID	2	g+s	File executes with group's privileges; on directory, new files inherit group
Sticky Bit	1	+t	On directory, only owner can delete/rename their files
bash
chmod 4755 /usr/bin/passwd     # SUID
chmod 2775 /shared             # SGID on directory
chmod 1777 /tmp                # Sticky bit
ls -l /usr/bin/passwd          # -rwsr-xr-x
ls -ld /tmp                    # drwxrwxrwt
