🧪 Lab: Create Groups and Users
Objective: Create three groups (devops, developers, operations), create users, and assign them appropriately. Then set up a shared directory with SGID.

Step 1: Create groups
bash
sudo groupadd devops
sudo groupadd developers
sudo groupadd operations
Verify:

bash
grep -E 'devops|developers|operations' /etc/group
Step 2: Create users
alice → primary group devops, supplementary developers

bob → primary group developers, supplementary operations

carol → primary group operations, supplementary devops

bash
sudo useradd -m -g devops -G developers alice
sudo useradd -m -g developers -G operations bob
sudo useradd -m -g operations -G devops carol

# Set passwords
echo "alice:Password123" | sudo chpasswd
echo "bob:Password123"   | sudo chpasswd
echo "carol:Password123" | sudo chpasswd
Verify:

bash
id alice
id bob
id carol
Step 3: Create a shared directory with SGID
bash
sudo mkdir -p /shared/dev-project
sudo chown root:developers /shared/dev-project
sudo chmod 2775 /shared/dev-project
ls -ld /shared/dev-project
Now files created inside will inherit the developers group.

Step 4: Test access
bash
su - alice
touch /shared/dev-project/alice.txt
ls -l /shared/dev-project/alice.txt   # Should show group developers
exit

su - bob
touch /shared/dev-project/bob.txt
exit

su - carol
touch /shared/dev-project/carol.txt   # Should fail if carol not in developers
exit
Step 5: Cleanup (optional)
bash
sudo userdel -r alice bob carol
sudo groupdel devops developers operations
sudo rm -rf /shared/dev-project
🔥 Troubleshooting: Permission Issue
Scenario: A user cannot read a file they should have access to.

Step 1: Reproduce the issue
bash
# As root, create a file owned by root with 600
sudo touch /tmp/secret.txt
sudo chmod 600 /tmp/secret.txt

# As alice, try to read
su - alice
cat /tmp/secret.txt
# Output: Permission denied
Step 2: Diagnose
bash
# Check file permissions and ownership
ls -l /tmp/secret.txt
# -rw------- 1 root root ... /tmp/secret.txt

# Check current user
id
# uid=1001(alice) gid=1001(devops) groups=1001(devops),1002(developers)

# Check directory permissions
ls -ld /tmp
Root cause: File is owned by root and only root has read/write. Alice has no permissions.

Step 3: Fix
Option A: Change ownership

bash
sudo chown alice:alice /tmp/secret.txt
Option B: Adjust permissions

bash
sudo chmod 640 /tmp/secret.txt
sudo chgrp developers /tmp/secret.txt
# Now alice (in developers) can read
Option C: Use ACLs (more granular)

bash
sudo setfacl -m u:alice:r /tmp/secret.txt
getfacl /tmp/secret.txt
Step 4: Verify
bash
su - alice
cat /tmp/secret.txt   # Should succeed
exit
Debug Chain for Permissions
text
ls -l <file>          # Check owner, group, mode
id                    # Check your UID/GID/groups
namei -l <path>       # Check permissions on each path component
getfacl <file>        # Check ACLs (if used)
sudo -l               # Check sudo rights
