Specific Answers
Difference between 755, 644, and 700?

Mode	Owner	Group	Others	Typical Use
755	rwx	r-x	r-x	Executables, directories
644	rw-	r--	r--	Regular files, configs
700	rwx	---	---	Private directories, scripts
How does sudo work?

User runs sudo <command>.

System checks /etc/sudoers (and /etc/sudoers.d/) for permission.

If allowed, user authenticates with their own password (unless NOPASSWD).

Command runs with target user's privileges (usually root).

Action is logged to /var/log/auth.log or /var/log/secure.

What is SUID?

SUID (Set User ID) is a special permission that allows a file to execute with the privileges of the file's owner, not the user running it.

Example: /usr/bin/passwd has SUID root so users can change their own passwords (which requires writing to /etc/shadow).

Numeric: 4 prefix (e.g., 4755). Symbolic: u+s.

Security risk: If a SUID binary has a vulnerability, it can be exploited to gain root.

📝 Notes & Reflections
Key Takeaways
Users and groups are the foundation of Linux access control.
Always follow least privilege — use sudo instead of logging in as root.
chmod numeric mode is fast; symbolic is more readable for complex changes.
SUID/SGID/sticky bit are powerful but must be used carefully.
umask controls default permissions — set it appropriately for the environment.

